# Cloudflare Pages redirect rules. First match wins, so order matters.
#
# Two jobs in one file:
#   1. the four screens are real URLs and must be served, not 404'd
#   2. the old ASP.NET CMS URLs are in Google's index and must land somewhere

# --- 1. THE FOUR SCREENS ---------------------------------------------------
#
# The site is one document with four client-side screens. The nav now pushes
# these paths, so a visitor can bookmark one, a crawler can index one, and a
# person can arrive at one directly -- but only if Pages serves the app under
# that path. 200 is a REWRITE: the URL stays as typed and index.html answers.
# A 301 here would bounce the visitor back to / and undo the whole point.
#
# The target is "/", NOT "/index.html". Pages normalises /index.html to /
# with a 308, so a rewrite aimed at /index.html gets caught by that and the
# visitor is bounced to the homepage -- measured: /about, /projects and
# /contact all returned 308 Location: / on both the domain and pages.dev.
#
# "/" needs no rule; it resolves to index.html on its own.

/about        /   200
/projects     /   200
/contact      /   200

# --- 2. THE OLD CMS URLS ---------------------------------------------------
#
# The old ASP.NET site published seven real pages, all still indexed and all
# 404 the moment Pages answers for the domain. Verified against the old origin
# (192.82.59.133 with a Host header): each of these returns 200 there.
#
# Where the new site has a matching screen, the redirect names it -- landing on
# the right screen beats landing on the homepage. Where it does not, "/" is the
# honest target rather than a screen that does not hold that content.

/home                 /          301
/about-us             /about     301
/our-certifications   /about     301
/contact-us           /contact   301

# Services remains a section of the home screen.
/services             /          301

# NOTE: /projects is deliberately absent from this list. It used to redirect to
# "/" and now it is a real URL, served by the rewrite above. Putting it back
# here would 301 the new page away.

# --- 3. OLD CMS SUBTREES ---------------------------------------------------
#
# A splat does not match its own bare path, so each needs both forms --
# /mobile was 404 for two days while /mobile/* worked, and /mobile is what
# Google returns first for "longmotive".

/gal          /  301
/gal/*        /  301
/cont         /  301
/cont/*       /  301
/usr          /  301
/usr/*        /  301

# The whole /prod/ tree was placeholder content -- category-01..03/021..023 and
# testing-product-1..12. Nothing there was ever real.
/prod         /  301
/prod/*       /  301

# The CMS served a parallel mobile site at /mobile/<same paths>.
/mobile       /  301
/mobile/*     /  301

# /data/* is deliberately absent: those are old CMS media files. A 404 is a
# truer answer for a missing image than a 301 to an HTML page.
